+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a duration into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A duration is either a whole number
+of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit, such as `1h30m`. The units are `d`
+(days), `h` (hours), `m` (minutes), and `s` (seconds). Each unit may appear at most once and in that order, and units
+are case-insensitive. Spaces, negative numbers, and decimals are not accepted. A {class}`~datetime.timedelta` passed as a
+default or from Python is returned unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    import datetime
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default=datetime.timedelta(minutes=5))
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    println()
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=120'])
+    println()
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    println()
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
